  "headline": "IPCC members reach impasse over timeline for next climate science review",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Members of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC), the world’s leading authority on climate change, have been unable to agree on a timeline to publish its next review of the latest science regarding global warming. According to interviews with more than a dozen current and former IPCC scientists, government negotiators, and meeting observers, the debate over publication dates has continued for nearly three years. Such assessments from the IPCC have informed every major United Nations climate agreement for three decades. The impasse could result in countries deciding on future phases of action without a consensus on critical issues, including the extent to which some global warming might be reversible. The dispute reflects rifts between wealthy industrialized nations, major emerging economies, and poorer nations concerning how to respond to warming temperatures and how much of the burden should be shouldered by different countries."
    }
  ]
}

