  "headline": "Separatist sentiment addressed at United Conservative Party annual general meeting in Alberta",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "During the United Conservative Party’s 2025 annual general meeting in Edmonton, Alberta, a member of the party raised the issue of provincial independence. While sitting on stage with her cabinet, Premier Danielle Smith was approached by Jeff Rath, a constitutional lawyer, UCP member, and figure in the Alberta independence movement. Rath asked the room how many members favored a free and independent Alberta, an inquiry that was met with an ovation from the crowd. Smith responded by stating, 'Well, I support an independent Alberta within a united Canada.'"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Communities oppose waste-to-energy plant near Dhaka, Bangladesh",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Construction of a $467 million power plant designed to incinerate municipal waste for electricity near Dhaka, Bangladesh, is facing opposition from nearby communities over potential health risks from toxic air pollution, according to a report by Eyamin Sajid for Mongabay. The project, which is being overseen by the Dhaka North City Corporation and owned by the China Machinery Engineering Corporation (CMEC), is slated for completion in December 2028. The facility is intended to incinerate up to 3,000 metric tons of waste daily to generate up to 42.5 megawatts of electricity. Densely populated communities and several schools are located less than a mile from the site. Local farmers, including rice and vegetable grower Md Naim, told Mongabay that unmanaged trash fires at the current dumpsite already cause headaches and breathing difficulties due to smoke and ash."
    },
    {
      "text": "An environmental impact assessment for the project states the incinerator will produce 74.8 metric tons of airborne fly ash daily. Additionally, an analysis by environmental groups indicates the plant will generate 39.56 metric tons of toxic substances annually, including heavy metals, dioxins, and furans, alongside 73,576 metric tons of solid waste. Hasan Mehedi, secretary of the Bangladesh Working Group on Ecology and Development (BWGED), stated that regular operation of the plant could lead to respiratory and lung-related problems, eye irritation, fatigue, and long-term health issues for local residents, particularly children and the elderly, due to chronic exposure to air pollution."
    }
  ]
}

