[
  {"text": "Iraq has rejected a request from Syria to repatriate approximately 4,000 Syrian detainees suspected of links to the Islamic State (ISIS), according to Iraqi National Security Advisor Qasim al-Aboudi. The detainees were transferred from Syrian detention facilities to Iraqi custody earlier this year. Al-Aboudi told The Associated Press that Damascus requested the Syrian nationals be handed over for trial in Syria, but Baghdad declined the request. A recent meeting involving Iraqi, Syrian, and U.S. officials addressed the fate of the detainees. Al-Aboudi stated that hundreds of the Syrians currently held in Iraq are believed to be senior members of ISIS. He noted that Iraq is conducting investigations and that anyone proven to have committed crimes in Iraq will be tried under Iraqi law. Al-Aboudi added that detainees from countries that do not impose the death penalty would not face capital punishment in Iraq, and those convicted could potentially be transferred to their home countries to serve remaining sentences if prisoner-transfer agreements are in place."} ,
  {"text": "Regarding the status of other detainees, al-Aboudi said Iraq has already returned approximately 50 Syrian detainees after determining they were not members of ISIS. Iraqi authorities have also identified about 400 Syrian detainees who were not affiliated with the group and could be released. Additionally, al-Aboudi stated that Baghdad has requested $500 million from the United States and other members of the international coalition against ISIS to assist with detention and prison-security costs. While the coalition has expressed willingness to provide support, Iraq has not yet received the funding. This detainee issue follows the end of the United States' 12-year military mission against ISIS in Iraq, with the final U.S. troops withdrawing from the country at the end of September."}
]
