{
  "headline": "Probe panel recommends suspension of four students following attack on journalists at Rajshahi University, Bangladesh",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "A five-member probe committee at Rajshahi University (RU) in Bangladesh has recommended the suspension of four students following an October 4 attack on journalists at Shaheed Ziaur Rahman Hall. The attack, which occurred while journalists were performing professional duties, resulted in injuries to at least 10 people, including four journalists, according to Vice-Chancellor Prof Dr Md Faridul Islam. The university administration received the investigation report around midnight on Thursday, and the Vice-Chancellor presented the findings to journalists on Friday. The committee was originally formed by the university administration to identify those involved in the incident after a meeting with journalists, with the Vice-Chancellor directing a report be submitted within 72 hours. The probe panel recommended the suspension of Ahsan Habib, Vice President of the RU unit of Jatiyatabadi Chhatra Dal (JCD); Hasibul Hasan, Joint General Secretary; Ariful Islam, General Secretary of the Ziaur Rahman Hall Students’ Union; and JCD activist Bishal Chakraborty."
    },
    {
      "text": "In addition to the recommended suspensions, the committee advised further investigation into allegations against four other students before taking action: Rakibul Islam, assistant cultural affairs secretary of the RU Central Students’ Union (RUCSU); Takbir Ahmed Emon, vice president of the RU unit of JCD; Syed Sajharul Islam, joint general secretary; and JCD activist Tanim Rahman. The committee also recommended issuing warnings to Bulbul Rahman and Shariful Islam, both vice-presidents of the university unit of JCD, and Shahriar Alam, an assistant office secretary."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Ashaiman Municipal Assembly lobbies for completion of storm-drain project in Ghana",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Ashaiman Municipal Assembly in Ghana is appealing to authorities, including the Hydrological Services Agency, to complete a storm-drain project at the Gbemi Stream to mitigate flood risks. Freeman Tsekpo, the Municipal Chief Executive (MCE), stated that funding is the primary obstacle to finishing the construction. According to the Assembly, only approximately 150 metres of the planned storm drain approaching the Starlight Bridge has been completed since work began following the construction of the bridge in 2020, leaving a critical section of the watercourse unconstructed. The Assembly noted that periodic desilting of the existing drain does not provide a permanent solution to the municipality's flooding challenges. Ashaiman has experienced major flooding in 2010, with further incidents reported in 2018 and 2024. Mr. Tsekpo noted that extensive dredging and desilting at the Ajei Kojo underbridge helped prevent major flooding during heavy rains in 2025 and 2026, but emphasized that such measures cannot replace a properly engineered storm-drain system."
    },
    {
      "text": "The Gbemi Stream also faces challenges from indiscriminate dumping. During a recent Municipal Assembly clean-up exercise, workers and machines were deployed to remove refuse from the watercourse. Mr. Tsekpo reported that residents living along the drain have used parts of it as a refuse dump, which reduces the capacity of the watercourse and increases the risk of obstruction during heavy rainfall. The Assembly is currently using additional workers and machinery to remove the refuse from the site."
    }
  ]
}
